WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.996
Treat a token as a claim that must be evaluated in
context.

00:00:03.996 --> 00:00:10.363
Verify its issuer, cryptographic protection,
audience and time constraints, then authorize the

00:00:10.363 --> 00:00:11.040
operation.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.052
Follow access and refresh tokens separately through
expiry and compromise.

00:00:16.052 --> 00:00:22.826
Central issuance and local verification offer a
useful pattern, but revocation and key rotation must

00:00:22.826 --> 00:00:24.993
be explicit rather than assumed.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.771
Apply security at the service as well as the
perimeter.

00:00:28.771 --> 00:00:35.627
Reduce runtime privileges, expose only needed
interfaces and manage secrets through their
lifecycle.

00:00:35.627 --> 00:00:42.278
Validate data for its actual destination: SQL, HTML
and outbound URLs require different controls.

00:00:42.278 --> 00:00:49.065
Shared middleware can make checks consistent, but
its trust assumptions and execution order must be

00:00:49.065 --> 00:00:50.574
understood and tested.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.632
Use simulations to practice decisions as well as
detections.

00:00:54.632 --> 00:01:00.815
Tabletop exercises, broad red-team engagements and
focused repeatable tests answer different

00:01:00.815 --> 00:01:01.487
questions.

00:01:01.487 --> 00:01:06.594
Our course exercise stays with synthetic evidence
and role-based discussion.

00:01:06.594 --> 00:01:12.978
Trace escalation, containment and recovery, then
assign concrete improvements after the review.

00:01:12.978 --> 00:01:17.951
Coverage tracking is useful evidence, not a
guarantee of future detection.
